{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"159831e6dafec53d","type":"text","text":"## While Loop\n\nA `while` loop iteratively evaluate condition and then execute the loop body, until condition is `False`.\n\n```run-python\ncount = 5\nwhile count > 0:\n\tprint(count)\n\tcount -= 1 #equivalent to count = count - 1\n```\n","x":-20,"y":-1500,"width":695,"height":314},
		{"id":"18c0f6d2a7e74d92","type":"text","text":"## Nested Loops\n\nA **nested loop** is a loop placed inside the body of another loop. \n\n```python\nmatrix = [\n    [1, 2, 3],\n    [4, 5, 6],\n    [7, 8, 9]\n]\n\n# Process each row, then each item inside that row\nfor row in matrix:\n    for item in row:\n        print(item, end=\" \")\n    print()  # Prints a newline after completing an entire row\n\n```\n","x":-20,"y":-1186,"width":695,"height":535},
		{"id":"62e47ed5d8ce9c80","type":"text","text":"## For Loop\nThe `for` loop executes a block of code a set number of times. It directly steps through elements in an iterable collection like lists, strings, dictionaries, or ranges.\n\n```python\n#Prints 0 to 4\nfor i in range(5):\n\tprint(i)\n```","x":-20,"y":-651,"width":695,"height":332},
		{"id":"997c082d3e0cc0e3","type":"text","text":"## Range() \nThe Python **`range()`** function generates an **immutable sequence of integers** on the fly, which is most commonly used to control the number of iterations in a `for` loop.\n\n`range(start, stop, step)`\n\n| Syntax                     | Behavior                                                          | Example           | Resulting Sequence |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------ |\n| `range(stop)`              | Starts at `0`, increments by `1`, stops **before** `stop`.        | `range(5)`        | `0, 1, 2, 3, 4`    |\n| `range(start, stop)`       | Starts at `start`, increments by `1`, stops **before** `stop`.    | `range(2, 6)`     | `2, 3, 4, 5`       |\n| `range(start, stop, step)` | Starts at `start`, increments by `step`, stops **before** `stop`. | `range(1, 10, 2)` | `1, 3, 5, 7, 9`    |\n","x":-20,"y":-319,"width":695,"height":627},
		{"id":"28b645d84dfe1f91","type":"text","text":"## Break and Continue\nThe **`break`** completely terminates the current loop, while **`continue`** skips the rest of the current iteration and jumps directly to the next cycle. Both statements work inside `for` and `while` loops.\n\n```python\n# Stops printing as soon as it hits 5\nfor num in range(1, 10):\n    if num == 5:\n        break\n    print(num)\n\n# Output: 1, 2, 3, 4\n```\n\n```python\n# Skips printing 5, but continues with 6, 7...\nfor num in range(1, 6):\n    if num == 3:\n        continue\n    print(num)\n\n# Output: 1, 2, 4, 5\n\n```\n","x":-20,"y":308,"width":695,"height":746},
		{"id":"83da8c92f4feafbb","type":"text","text":"## If statements\n\nIf the condition is `True`, executes the respective statements.\n![[Control Structures-1781613067896.webp|516]]","x":-760,"y":-691,"width":700,"height":515},
		{"id":"cdfe8ab36190c1b8","type":"text","text":"## If-else Statement\n\nIf the condition is `True` execute statement_1; otherwise execute statement_2\n\n![[Control Structures-1781613355559.webp|512]]\n","x":-760,"y":-176,"width":700,"height":565},
		{"id":"922bd0769e42b6d7","type":"text","text":"## If-elif Statement\n\n```python\nscore = 85\n\nif score >= 90:\n    print(\"Grade: A\")\nelif score >= 80:\n    print(\"Grade: B\")  # This block executes because 85 >= 80 is True\nelif score >= 70:\n    print(\"Grade: C\")  # Skipped, even though 85 >= 70 is also True\nelse:\n    print(\"Grade: F\")  # Catch-all fallback block\n\n```","x":-757,"y":389,"width":695,"height":486},
		{"id":"faa7d639bba0ab2f","type":"text","text":"## Relation Operations\nReturns `Boolean` (`True` or `False`)\n```python\nx > y   #x greater than y\nx >= y  #x greater than or equal to y\nx < y   #x lesser than y\nx <= y  #x lesser than or equal to y\nx == y  #x is equal to y (not same as =)\nx != y  #x is not equal to y\n```","x":-757,"y":-1040,"width":700,"height":349},
		{"id":"5dcf751d5929f173","type":"text","text":"## Logical Operations\n![[Basic Data Types-1781598279920.webp]]","x":-760,"y":-1500,"width":700,"height":460},
		{"id":"7d7c1de0c4e3652a","type":"text","text":"## Mathematical Operations\n\n```python\nprint(7 + 3)   #Addition\nprint(7 - 3)   #Subraction\nprint(7 * 3)   #Multiplication\nprint(7 / 3)   #Division\nprint(25 // 6) #Quotient - Floor Division\nprint(25 % 6)  #Remainder - Modulo Operator\nprint(6 ** 2)  #Power\n```\n","x":-1500,"y":-1500,"width":700,"height":344},
		{"id":"84d712912a4e387b","type":"text","text":"# Tuple\n\nA **tuple** is a built-in collection data type used to store multiple items in a single variable. The core definition of a tuple is that it is **ordered**, **immutable (unchangeable)**, and **allows duplicate values**. \n## Tuple Operations\n\n| **Operations** | **Meaning**                                                                                                        |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `a[i]`         | returns the i-th element of a                                                                                      |\n| `a[i:j:k]`     | returns a tuple consisting of elements of a from index $i$ (**inclusive**) to $j$ (**exclusive**), in steps of $k$ |\n| `len(a)`       | returns the number of elements in a                                                                                |\n| `min(a)`       | returns the smallest element in a                                                                                  |\n| `max(a)`       | returns the largest element in a                                                                                   |\n| `x in a`       | returns **True** if x is an element of a, or **False** otherwise                                                   |\n| `a + b`        | returns a new tuple concatenating a and b                                                                          |\n| `n * a`        | returns a new tuple that is n copies of a                                                                          |\n### Membership Testing (`in`/`not in`)\n```run-python\ncolors = (\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\")\n\nprint(\"green\" in colors)      # Output: True\nprint(\"yellow\" not in colors)  # Output: True\n\n```\n\n### Iterating Through a Tuple\n```run-python\nbrands = (\"Apple\", \"Google\", \"Microsoft\")\n\nfor brand in brands:\n    print(brand)\n\n```\n\n","x":2220,"y":-1500,"width":700,"height":940},
		{"id":"477da6d6a85acfc6","type":"text","text":"# List\n\nA **Python list** is a built-in, mutable, and ordered collection of elements enclosed in square brackets `[]`. It allows you to store items of **different data types** in a single variable, and supports duplicate values\n## Accessing Elements\n\n```python\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\", \"date\"]\n#Index:      0         1         2        3\n#Index:     -4        -3        -2       -1\n\nprint(fruits[0])    # Output: \"apple\"\nprint(fruits[-1])   # Output: \"date\" (negative indexing)\nprint(fruits[1:3])  # Output: ('banana', 'cherry') (slicing)\n```\n\n## List Operations\n\n| **Operations** | **Meaning**                                                      |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `x in a`       | returns **True** if x is an element of a, or **False** otherwise |\n| `a + b`        | returns a new list concatenating a and b                         |\n| `n * a`        | returns a new list that is n copies of a                         |\n\n### Modifying and Altering Elements\n\n| Operations          | **Meaning / Description**                                                               |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `list.append(x)`    | Adds an element `x` to the end of the list.                                             |\n| `list.insert(i, x)` | Inserts an element `x` at a specific index `i`.                                         |\n| `list.remove(x)`    | Removes the first occurrence of element `x`. Raises a `ValueError` if not found.        |\n| `list.pop([i])`     | Removes and returns the element at index `i`. If `i` is omitted, removes the last item. |\n| `list.clear()`      | Removes all elements from the list, leaving it empty.                                   |\n\n### Searching and Information Retrieval\n\n| **Syntax**      | **Meaning / Description**                                                                        |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `len(list)`     | Global function that returns the total number of elements in the list.                           |\n| `list.count(x)` | Returns the total number of times element `x` appears in the list.                               |\n| `list.index(x)` | Returns the zero-based index of the first occurrence of `x`. Raises a `ValueError` if not found. |\n| `min(list)`     | Global function that returns the smallest element in the list.                                   |\n| `max(list)`     | Global function that returns the largest element in the list.                                    |\n| `sum(list)`     | Global function that returns the sum of all elements (works on lists of numbers).                |\n\n### Ordering and Utility\n\n| **Syntax**                 | **Meaning / Description**                                                                |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `list.sort(reverse=False)` | Sorts the list items **in-place** (modifies the original list).                          |\n| `sorted(list)`             | Global function that returns a **new** sorted list, leaving the original list unchanged. |\n| `list.reverse()`           | Reverses the elements of the list **in-place**.                                          |\n| `list.copy()`              | Returns a shallow copy of the list.                                                      |\n","x":2960,"y":-1500,"width":700,"height":1599},
		{"id":"9b47aa67caf50647","type":"text","text":"# Common Python Built in Functions\n\n## 🔢 Mathematical & Numeric\n\n| Function                  | Description                                                   |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `abs(-5) # 5`             | Returns the absolute value of a given number.                 |\n| `max([1, 5, 2]) # 5`      | Returns the largest item in an iterable or among arguments.   |\n| `min([1, 5, 2]) # 1`      | Returns the smallest item in an iterable or among arguments.  |\n| `pow(2, 3) # 8`           | Raises a base number to the power of an exponent (\\(x^{y}\\)). |\n| `round(3.1415, 2) # 3.14` | Rounds a floating-point number to a specified precision.      |\n| `sum([1, 2, 3]) # 6`      | Sums up the start value and all items of an iterable.         |\n\n## 🗂️ Data Types & Type Casting\n\n| Function                  | Description                                                  |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `dict(a=1, b=2)`          | Instantiates a built-in dictionary collection object.        |\n| `float(\"3.14\") # 3.14`    | Converts a number or a string into a floating-point decimal. |\n| `int(\"42\") # 42`          | Converts a number or string into a base-10 whole integer.    |\n| `list((1, 2)) # [1, 2]`   | Creates a dynamic mutable sequence list object.              |\n| `obj = object()`          | Returns a new featureless base object instance.              |\n| `set([1, 1, 2]) # {1, 2}` | Creates an unordered collection of distinct hashable values. |\n| `str(10) # '10'`          | Converts any target object into its formal string version.   |\n| `tuple([1, 2]) # (1, 2)`  | Creates an unchangeable immutable sequence tuple object.     |\n| `type(5) # <class 'int'>` | Returns the active data type class of the queried object.    |\n","x":720,"y":-1486,"width":700,"height":786},
		{"id":"473dbc98ab38bae3","type":"text","text":"## Indexing of Strings\n\n| Character          | P    | y    | t    | h    | o    | n    |\n| ------------------ | ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- |\n| **Positive Index** | `0`  | `1`  | `2`  | `3`  | `4`  | `5`  |\n| **Negative Index** | `-6` | `-5` | `-4` | `-3` | `-2` | `-1` |\n","x":1480,"y":-1491,"width":700,"height":243},
		{"id":"fcf2a799078118b6","type":"text","text":"# Dictionary\nA **Python dictionary** is a built-in, mutable data structure that stores data in **ordered key-value pairs**, where each unique key maps to a specific value.\n## Basic Syntax\n\n```run-python\n# Empty dictionary\nuser_info = {} \n\n# Dictionary with initial data\nuser_info = {\n    \"username\": \"coder123\",\n    \"email\": \"coder123@example.com\",\n    \"login_count\": 5\n}\n```\n\n## Dictionary Operations\n\n| Operations               | Description                                                                               |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `d[\"key\"]`               | returns value associated with the `key` (Raises `KeyError` if key does not exist)         |\n| `d.get(\"key\",\"default\")` | returns value associated with the `key` (Returns `None` or a default value)               |\n| `d[\"key\"] = \"new_value\"` | modifies the value associated with the key                                                |\n| `d[\"new_key\"] = value`   | adds a new key if it does not exist                                                       |\n| `d.pop(\"key\",\"default\")` | remove key-value pair and returns the value (returns default value if key does not exist) |\n| `del d[\"key\"]`           | deletes key-value pair without returning                                                  |\n| `d.clear()`              | Removes all elements from the dictionary.                                                 |\n| `d.items()`              | Returns a view object containing the `(key, value)` tuple pairs.                          |\n| `d.keys()`               | Returns a view object list of all keys in the dictionary.                                 |\n| `d.values()`             | Returns a view object list of all values in the dictionary.                               |\n| `d.copy()`               | Returns a shallow copy of the dictionary.                                                 |\n## Iterating Through Dictionaries\n\n```run-python\nuser_info = {\n    \"username\": \"coder123\",\n    \"email\": \"coder123@example.com\",\n    \"login_count\": 5\n}\n\n# Loop through keys and values simultaneously\nfor key, value in user_info.items():\n    print(f\"{key}: {value}\")\n\n# Loop through keys only\nfor key in user_info.keys():\n    print(key)\n\n# Loop through values only\nfor val in user_info.values():\n    print(val)\n```\n","x":3700,"y":-1500,"width":700,"height":1420},
		{"id":"70ca15220d2ea7e6","x":4420,"y":-1500,"width":646,"height":2180,"type":"text","text":"# Recursion\n\n## Factorial\n\n```run-python\ndef factorial(n):\n    # 1. Base Case (Stops the loop)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # 2. Recursive Case (Calls itself with smaller input)\n    else:\n        return n * factorial(n - 1)\n\nprint(factorial(5))  # Output: 120\n```\n\n### Visualising the Call Stack\n\nWhen you run `factorial(3)`, Python uses an internal memory structure called the **Call Stack**. It pushes active function instances onto the stack until it reaches the base case, then unwinds them in a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) order:\n\nSTACK PUSHING (Going Down)         STACK UNWINDING (Returning Up)\n  factorial(3)                                            3 * factorial(2) -> 3 * 2 = 6\n    └── factorial(2)                                        2 * factorial(1) -> 2 * 1 = 2\n          └── factorial(1)                                    Returns 1 (Base Case reached)\n\n## Fibonacci\n\n```run-python\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    # Base cases: return n if it is 0 or 1\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    # Recursive case: sum the previous two terms\n    else:\n        return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)\n\n# Example: Get the 7th Fibonacci number (0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13...)\nprint(fibonacci(7))  # Output: 13\n```\n\n### Trace Tree\n\n```python\n               fibonacci(4)\n               /          \\\n       fibonacci(3)      fibonacci(2)\n       /          \\          /        \\\n fibonacci(2)  fib(1)    fib(1)      fib(0)\n   /      \\       |        |           |\n fib(1)  fib(0)   1        1           0\n\n   |       |\n   1       0\n\n```\n\n## Other Examples\n\n### Super Fibonacci\n\nA super-Fibonacci sequence (SFS) is a list of integers with the property that, from the third term onwards, every term is the sum of all the previous terms.\n$$\n\t1, 5, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96, 192, 384, 768\n\t\n$$\n$$\n\t1, 6, 7, 14, 28, 56, 112, 224, 448, 896\n$$\nAssume the first term is always 1, the recursive function `superFibonacciSeqR(t2,n)` returns the first `n` terms of an SFS with its second term as `t2`.\n\n```python\ndef superFibonacciSeqR(t2,n):\n    if n == 2:\n        return [1,t2]\n    elif n > 0:\n        return superFibonacciSeqR(t2,n-1) + [(1 + t2)*2**(n-3)]\n```\n\n### Swap\n\nGiven a list of n integer numbers, swap the number at index `i` (call this `x`) with the number at index `n-i-1` (call this `y`) if `i < n-i-1 and x < y`, for all valid `i`\n\nExample: `[1,20,2,4,7,3,10]` should result in `[10,20,7,4,2,3,1]`\n\n```python\ndef swaplist_rec(lst):\n    if len(lst) <= 1:\n        return lst     \n    else:\n        if lst[0] < lst[-1]:\n            return [lst[-1]] + swaplist_rec(lst[1:-1]) + [lst[0]]\n        else:\n            return [lst[0]] + swaplist_rec(lst[1:-1]) + [lst[-1]]\n\nl1 = [1,20,2,4,7,3,10]\n\nprint(swaplist_rec(l1))\n```"},
		{"id":"d7538c03c882282f","type":"text","text":"## List Comprehensions\n\n### Basic Syntax\n\n```python\nnew_list = [expression for item in iterable]\n```\n\n```run-python\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nsquares = [num ** 2 for num in numbers]\n# Output: [1, 4, 9, 16]\n\nprint(squares)\n```\n\n**Filtering with `if`**\n\n```run-python\n# Keep only even numbers\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nevens = [n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]\n# Output: [2, 4, 6]\n\nprint(evens)\n```\n\n**Transforming with `if-else` **\n\n```run-python\n# Label numbers as 'Even' or 'Odd'\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nlabels = [\"Even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"Odd\" for n in numbers]\n# Output: ['Odd', 'Even', 'Odd', 'Even']\n\nprint(labels)\n```\n\n**Nested Loops**\n\n```run-python\npairs = [(x, y) for x in range(2) for y in range(2)]\n# Output: [(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1)]\n\nprint(pairs)\n```\n\n**Flattening a 2D Matrix**\n\n```run-python\nmatrix = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nflat = [num for row in matrix for num in row]\n# Output: [1, 2, 3, 4]\n\nprint(flat)\n```\n","x":2960,"y":120,"width":700,"height":1120},
		{"id":"dd5260f95fb1edb4","type":"text","text":"## Key Components of Python Functions\n\n- **Arguments & Parameters**: Parameters are variables listed in the function definition. Arguments are the actual values sent to the function when called.\n\n- **Return Values**: Functions use the `return` statement to send data back to the caller. If no return is specified, it implicitly returns `None`.\n\n- **Default Parameters**: You can assign default values to parameters if an argument is omitted during the call.\n\n```run-python\n# Function with a default parameter and a return value\ndef calculate_total(price, tax=0.05):\n    return price + (price * tax)\n\n# Calling with one argument (uses default tax)\nprint(calculate_total(100))  # Output: 105.0\n\n# Calling with both arguments (overrides default tax)\nprint(calculate_total(100, 0.10))  # Output: 110.0\n\n```\n","x":720,"y":-664,"width":700,"height":488},
		{"id":"8a1a49883bef7465","type":"text","text":"## String Slicing\n\n```python\ntext = \"Python\"\n# Text:   P  y  t  h  o  n\n# Index:  0  1  2  3  4  5\n# Index: -6 -5 -4 -3 -2 -1\n\nprint(text[1:4])  # Output: \"yth\" (Indices 1, 2, 3)\nprint(text[:4])   # Output: \"Pyth\" (Indices 0 to 3)\nprint(text[2:])   # Output: \"thon\" (Indices 2 to 5)\nprint(text[:])    # Output: \"Python\"\nprint(text[::2])  # Output: \"Pto\" (Every 2nd character)\nprint(text[-3:])  # Output: \"hon\" (Last 3 characters)\nprint(text[::-1])  # Output: \"nohtyP\"\nprint(text[-4::-1])  #Output: \"tyP\"\nprint(text[-1:-5:-1])  #Output: \"noht\"\n```","x":1480,"y":-1220,"width":700,"height":420},
		{"id":"6a97efa95c040f38","type":"text","text":"## String Comparison\n\nPython evaluates string values character by character using their underlying **Unicode code point values**.\n\n```python\nstr1 = \"apple\"\nstr2 = \"banana\"\n\nprint(str1 == str2)  # False\nprint(str1 != str2)  # True\nprint(str1 < str2)   # True (because 'a' comes before 'b')\n```\n","x":1480,"y":-760,"width":700,"height":300},
		{"id":"1b1f0596a60b1fb6","type":"text","text":"## Essential String Methods\n| Operation            | Method Syntax                                  | Example Output                                                    |\n| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Modify Case**      | `text.upper()` / `text.lower()`/`text.title()` | `\"PYTHON\"` / `\"python\"`/`\"Python\"`                                |\n| **Trim Spaces**      | `text.strip()`                                 | Removes leading/trailing spaces                                   |\n| **Substitutions**    | `text.replace(\"old\", \"new\")`                   | Replaces character patterns                                       |\n| **Find Position**    | `text.find(\"target\")`                          | Index integer, or `-1` if missing                                 |\n| **Split to List**    | `text.split(\",\")`                              | `['item1', 'item2']`                                              |\n| **Join List**        | `\"-\".join(['A', 'B'])`                         | `\"A-B\"`                                                           |\n| Count                | `text.count(\"are\")`                            | Outputs the number of `\"are\"` (case sensitive)                    |\n| Check Start and End  | `text.startswith(\"\")`/ `text.endswith(\"\")`     | Checks starts/end of `text` (Boolean)                             |\n| Check Digit/Alphabet | `text.isdigit()`\\ `text.isalpha()`             | Checks if `text` is all digits or alphabet respectively (Boolean) |\n","x":1480,"y":-432,"width":700,"height":492},
		{"id":"f5f575b3ad724dc4","type":"text","text":"## String Operations\n\n| **Operations** | **Meaning**                                                                                                              |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `a[i]`         | returns the character at index $i$                                                                                       |\n| `a[i:j:k]`     | returns a substring consisting of characters of a from index $i$ (**inclusive**) to $j$ (**exclusive**), in steps of $k$ |\n| `len(a)`       | returns the number of characters in a                                                                                    |\n| `min(a)`       | returns the smallest character in a                                                                                      |\n| `max(a)`       | returns the largest character in a                                                                                       |\n| `x in a`       | returns `True` if x exists in a, or `False` otherwise                                                                    |\n| `a + b`        | returns a new string concatenating a and b                                                                               |\n| `n * a`        | returns a new string that is n copies of a                                                                               |\n\n","x":1480,"y":99,"width":700,"height":480},
		{"id":"f8ca7e947c13639f","type":"text","text":"## Iterating over Strings\n\n```python\ntext = \"Python\"\nfor char in text:\n    print(char)\n\n```\n\n**Iterating in reverse order**\n```python\ntext = \"Python\"\nfor char in reversed(text):\n    print(char)\n```\n\n**Iterating over words**\n```python\ntext = \"Learning Python is fun\"\nfor word in text.split():\n    print(word)\n```\n","x":1480,"y":635,"width":700,"height":605},
		{"id":"7fd1b8ec9da6d1c2","type":"text","text":"# Set\n\nAa **`set`** is a built-in data structure used to store an **unordered collection of unique elements**. Sets are mutable,  automatically filter out any duplicate values and do not track the order of elements\n\n## Basic Syntax\n\n```run-python\n# Creating a set with elements\nfruits = {\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\", \"apple\"} \nprint(fruits)  \n# Output: {'banana', 'cherry', 'apple'} \n#(Duplicates are auto-removed)\n\n# Creating an empty set\nempty_set = set() \n\n# Converting a list with duplicates into a unique set\nnumbers = set([1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4]) \nprint(numbers) # Output: {1, 2, 3, 4}\n```\n\n## Operations to Check Set Properties\n\n| **Operations** | **Meaning**                                                              |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `len(a)`       | returns the number of elements in a                                      |\n| `min(a)`       | returns the smallest element in a                                        |\n| `max(a)`       | returns the largest element in a                                         |\n| `x in a`       | returns **True** if x is an element of a, or **False** otherwise         |\n| `a.add()`      | adds a single element to the set                                         |\n| `a.update()`   | adds multiple elements to the set from another collection                |\n| `a.remove()`   | removes an element from the set (raises an error if item does not exist) |\n| `a.discard()`  | removes an element from the set (does nothing if item does not exist)    |\n\n## Mathematical Operations\n\n| Operation           | Method Syntax               | Operator Syntax     | What it does                                       |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Union**           | `a.union(b)`                | <code>a \\| b</code> | Combines elements from both sets.                  |\n| **Intersection**    | `a.intersection(b)`         | `a & b`             | Keeps only items present in both sets.             |\n| **Difference**      | `a.difference(b)`           | `a - b`             | Keeps items in `a` that are not in `b`.            |\n| **Symmetric Diff.** | `a.symmetric_difference(b)` | `a ^ b`             | Keeps items unique to each set (omits duplicates). |\n![[5. Collections (Part II)-1781699088047.webp]]\n","x":2220,"y":-520,"width":700,"height":1648}
	],
	"edges":[]
}